{"timestamp":"2026-07-18T15:27:29.869Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0bd6d73b974b1fab016a5b9b61de5c81919d761ae99275498f","status":"completed","call_id":"call_kyieT7bHk4k2oDjx0YymE9zu","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/thomi/Projects/entire-plan/ENTIRE-BRAIN-BENCHMARK-AND-PRODUCT-IMPROVEMENT-GOAL-2026-07-16.md && sed -n '640,926p' /Users/thomi/Projects/entire-plan/ENTIRE-BRAIN-BENCHMARK-AND-PRODUCT-IMPROVEMENT-GOAL-2026-07-16.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"5dfd74e8-4c67-4fb7-a4c3-45f47a5e848d"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-18T15:27:30.049Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_019f75d6-f701-76e3-acd3-c72bbcefffe2","call_id":"call_kyieT7bHk4k2oDjx0YymE9zu","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# Entire Brain benchmark and product-improvement goal\n\nStatus: active. This goal supersedes the benchmark-only completion definition in the earlier repair\nplan. The benchmark is an enabling system, not the terminal deliverable.\n\nOperational state moved on after this goal was written. The authoritative checkpoint is the\n[2026-07-18 handover](ENTIRE-BRAIN-AND-BRAIN-BENCH-HANDOVER-2026-07-18.md): Entire Brain PR #75 is\nclean and pushed at `923b8a915925967c8ee1ffab3ff603f688a5427b`, but conflicts with live `main`\n(120 commits ahead, 83 behind). It contains benchmark-gate infrastructure plus measured product-path\nimprovements to history retrieval, stale-index bridging, semantic-root dedupe, action relevance, Git\nintent, and test-suggestion ranking. Its final focused, race, full, and vet validation is green, but\nthe terminal three-endpoint goal is not met and reconciliation must be retested.\n\nBrain Bench development continued separately in draft PR #34 at `d2f13dc`. Its corrected\nthree-repository Claude Opus pilot is diagnostic only: Entire Graph used fewer output tokens, tools,\ntime, and reported cost but tied CBM on eligible acceptance at 1/3 and did not win correctness. A later\ntwo-case replay recorded 0/2 controller acceptance, although Vite remains a scorer-validity question\nand VS Code is a genuine behavioral failure. The branch now also implements an 18-case,\nthree-repository Tasks smoke whose nine-attempt Opus run was started by a separate pre-existing Codex\nlane at 12:36 CEST while this documentation audit was in progress. Its scoring semantics require unpaid\nrepair/review, its in-progress outputs are non-rankable diagnostics, and this docs task did not initiate\nor authorize it. The previous PR #34 CI run had one inherited\nfrozen-V8 closure failure; fresh CI at the new head was in progress. The 248-attempt Entire Brain schedule still\nhas no execution authority and no attempt ran. The separate Agent V1/V2 commits through `77ace7bd` are\nnot ancestors of PR #75 and must not be described as integrated there. About 191.9 GB remained available\nfor important use; the open resource issue is trusted APFS-aware reservation/cleanup attestation, not\ndisk fullness. No confirmatory provider, private-log, or holdout run is authorized.\n\nSections 8–9.22 below are a chronological engineering log through 2026-07-17. Their measurements remain\nuseful when tied to the named commits, but their “current” and “next” wording is superseded by the\nhandover. The mission, completion contract, endpoint definitions, statistical rule, optimization loop,\nguardrails, and owner-decision requirements remain normative.\n\n## 1. Mission\n\nConstruct a causal, reproducible benchmark for Entire Brain and use it as the control system for\niterative Entire Brain improvement. Benchmark construction and product improvement are both in scope;\nneither is the finish line by itself. The program is complete only when powered evidence shows\nstatistically defensible and practically meaningful gains in all three user-facing dimensions, with\nno material cross-dimension regression:\n\n1. less end-to-end elapsed time to a validated coding result;\n2. lower model-token cost for that result; and\n3. higher downstream code quality.\n\nBenchmark readiness, a better retrieval metric, or a favorable point estimate in only one or two\ndimensions is progress, not completion. Current serializer microbenchmarks, packet bytes, allocation\ncounts, lexical proxies, and transparent synthetic utility scores are diagnostic product evidence;\nthey are not powered proof of this finish line.\n\n## 2. Required final comparisons\n\nTwo comparisons must remain distinct.\n\n### 2.1 User-value comparison\n\nCompare the final optimized `retrieved_memory` treatment with treatment-neutral `no_memory` under the\nsame task, runner, effort, prompt contract, cache policy, and schedule. This is the primary product\nclaim. The final treatment must clear the frozen superiority rule for time, token cost, and code\nquality jointly.\n\n### 2.2 Product-progress comparison\n\nCompare the final optimized Entire Brain treatment with a content-addressed pre-optimization Entire\nBrain baseline. This establishes which engineering changes improved the product rather than merely\nre-describing the no-memory contrast. Each optimization cycle must report this comparison, including\npacket, retrieval, latency, usage, and validation deltas.\n\nThe product-progress comparison cannot replace the user-value comparison. A faster new implementation\nthat still fails to beat no memory in all three dimensions does not complete this goal.\n\n## 3. Non-negotiable completion contract\n\nThe goal is complete only when all of the following are true:\n\n1. The benchmark treatments are causally isolated: prompts are treatment-neutral, task prompts are\n   symptom-only, hidden validation stays hidden, temporal eligibility precedes ranking, order is\n   counterbalanced, and cache state is explicit.\n2. Engine, product, prompt, task, packet, model, runtime, pricing, and analyzer identities are\n   content-addressed and independently verifiable.\n3. Public benchmark evidence is relocatable and privacy-safe. Exact sensitive replay bytes, if\n   retained, are client-side encrypted and access-controlled; they are never treated as public\n   evidence merely because they are encrypted.\n4. The task population is representative across repositories, task families, difficulty, answerable\n   and null cases, and cold and warm product states. Development and holdout membership is unique and\n   contamination-tracked.\n5. Every co-primary observation has complete quality, elapsed-time, and normalized usage/cost data.\n   Missing usage, missing timing, invalid treatment identity, or incomplete validation fails closed.\n6. The statistical design is powered for the joint three-endpoint success rule using development-only\n   calibration or an explicitly approved conservative assumption set.\n7. The final optimized treatment beats no memory on all three co-primary endpoints, with the frozen\n   confidence bounds clearing both zero-effect and practical-effect thresholds and no material\n   cross-dimension regression hidden by aggregation.\n8. Prespecified critical strata show no material reversal, and the final candidate passes security,\n   correctness, compatibility, and benchmark-integrity regression suites.\n9. Product changes, benchmark inputs, raw evidence, analysis code, effect estimates, uncertainty, and\n   residual risks are documented well enough for an independent clean-checkout reproduction.\n\nIf any one co-primary endpoint fails, the outcome is `not yet complete`; the result must feed another\ndiagnosis and product-improvement cycle. There is no compensating composite score in which a large win\non one dimension can hide a loss on another.\n\n## 4. Co-primary endpoint contract\n\nThe final numeric floors are frozen only after unpaid development calibration and before the holdout\nis opened. They may be made stricter during development, but never weakened after confirmatory results\nare observed. The initial planning targets are a 10% elapsed-time reduction, a 12% model-cost\nreduction, and a 5 percentage-point normalized code-quality increase; these are planning values, not\nyet the frozen confirmatory thresholds.\n\n### 4.1 Elapsed time\n\nPrimary measurement: monotonic wall time beginning immediately before harness-owned treatment\nretrieval/delivery—or the same logical no-op for `no_memory`—through agent CLI completion, or through\nthe observed treatment-failure/timeout-termination boundary when no final response exists. The boundary\nis captured before usage parsing, hashing, and artifact writes; setup, secret preflight, hidden\nvalidation, and cleanup are separate diagnostics. A timed-out attempt retains its measured interval;\nan agent-component timeout limit is never substituted for it. A successful-attempt-only headline is\nprohibited.\n\nRequired diagnostics include cold/warm state, index readiness, retrieval latency, packet-build\nlatency, first-context latency, model/API time, retry time, and total harness time. These explain a\nresult but do not replace the end-to-end endpoint.\n\n### 4.2 Token cost\n\nPrimary measurement: total billed model cost over all calls needed for the attempt, using mutually\nexclusive uncached-input, cache-read, cache-write, visible-output, and reasoning-output categories with\nexplicit provider inclusion semantics and a frozen price or price-alias binding for every category.\nEntire-delivered context, retries, recovery calls, and failed attempts are included.\n\nRaw token categories and a price-independent normalized-token view are retained as secondary\ndiagnostics. Provider-specific inclusive counters must be normalized before aggregation so cached or\nreasoning tokens are not double counted. A successful-attempt-only cost headline is prohibited.\n\n### 4.3 Code quality\n\nPrimary measurement: a task-normalized score in `[0, 1]` specified before execution and derived from\nhidden functional validation, regression safety, task-contract adherence, and code-quality checks.\nCritical correctness, security, or build failures force the attempt score to zero. Machine-checkable\ncriteria are preferred; any human judgment is blinded to treatment, independently reviewed, and\nadjudicated under a frozen rubric.\n\nValidation pass rate, defect severity, unnecessary-edit count, focused-test discipline, and static\nanalysis are retained as diagnostics and safety gates. They cannot be substituted post hoc for the\nfrozen quality endpoint.\n\n## 5. Statistical decision rule\n\nThe unit of generalization is the task, not the individual repetition. Treatments are paired within\ntask and scheduled with deterministic counterbalancing. Repetitions estimate within-task execution\nnoise; they do not manufacture more independent tasks.\n\nThe primary claim uses an intersection-union rule: all three endpoint null hypotheses must be rejected\nin the favorable direction. Elapsed time uses a paired-task log ratio; normalized cost uses the ratio\nof equal-task-weighted task-arm mean costs; quality uses the paired-task mean normalized-score\ndifference. The locked analyzer reports point estimates, one-sided confidence bounds, raw\ndenominators, invalid/missing cells, and bootstrap or model diagnostics.\n\nThe powered design must declare, before confirmatory execution:\n\n- practical effect floors and direction for all three endpoints;\n- confidence level, resampling/model method, seed, and finite-sample corrections;\n- task and repetition counts, clustering, stratification, and missing/censored-cell policy;\n- one primary optimized-Entire-versus-no-memory contrast;\n- multiplicity treatment for additional product-baseline, engine, stratum, and diagnostic contrasts;\n- minimum power for the joint rule and the assumptions used to obtain it; and\n- no-material-regression bounds for critical task, repository, and cache-state strata.\n\nExploratory slices and successful-attempt-only diagnostics are allowed only when visibly labeled and\ncannot change the primary verdict.\n\n## 6. Benchmark-to-product optimization loop\n\nEvery development cycle follows the same evidence-preserving sequence:\n\n1. Freeze the current product binary, configuration, corpus, engine, task set, prompts, packets, and\n   analyzer as the cycle baseline.\n2. Run or reuse unpaid deterministic/retrieval diagnostics and authorized development cells only.\n3. Attribute losses to a measured stage: indexing, candidate eligibility, query construction,\n   lexical/semantic retrieval, rank fusion, diversity/deduplication, packet selection/serialization,\n   cache behavior, delivery/tool integration, or agent use of context.\n4. Form one falsifiable change hypothesis with expected effects on time, cost, and quality.\n5. Implement the smallest product change that tests it, with focused unit/integration tests and new\n   telemetry where attribution was previously impossible.\n6. Compare the candidate with both the frozen cycle baseline and no memory on development data.\n7. Reject candidates with a correctness/security regression or a material reversal in any\n   co-primary dimension. Retain only candidates that advance the three-dimensional Pareto frontier.\n8. Record the exact diff, artifact hashes, measurements, uncertainty, and decision before beginning\n   the next cycle.\n\nOptimization never uses fresh holdout outcomes. A failed development hypothesis is retained as useful\nnegative evidence rather than silently discarded.\n\n## 7. Execution plan\n\n### Phase A — trustworthy measurement substrate\n\n- Complete privacy-safe, relative-path v4 engine evidence.\n- Retain exact v3 bytes only through an approved encrypted forensic path; do not publish plaintext v3.\n- Verify all three engines, temporal eligibility, result ordering, candidate/vector commitments, and\n  artifact identity from a clean checkout.\n- Keep the holdout unopened and run no paid model cells.\n\nExit: the machine-engine gate can pass without private corpus text, session identity, host paths,\nsecrets, raw semantic vectors, runtime trees, or a redistributed Gemma model in public evidence.\n\n### Phase B — three-dimensional metric and instrumentation contract\n\n- Replace correctness non-inferiority plus secondary time with co-primary quality, cost, and time\n  superiority.\n- Extend run schemas and the harness with normalized cost, end-to-end timing boundaries, product-stage\n  timing, cache state, packet measurements, retry/failure accounting, and predeclared quality scores.\n- Extend the locked analyzer, power model, protocol checker, and adversarial tests.\n\nExit: a synthetic balanced suite produces all three paired endpoints, fails closed on missing or\ntampered fields, and cannot declare success unless every endpoint clears its practical bound.\n\n### Phase C — representative development population and baselines\n\n- Expand symptom-only tasks across repositories, task families, difficulty levels, nulls, and\n  cold/warm states; independently review task validity and contamination.\n- Capture the frozen no-memory and pre-optimization Entire Brain baselines.\n- Run deterministic retrieval/relevance diagnostics and only explicitly authorized development model\n  calibration.\n\nExit: variance, failure modes, and bottlenecks are measured on enough independent tasks to choose a\npowered design; the confirmatory holdout remains sealed.\n\n### Phase D — product-improvement sprints\n\n- Prioritize measured hot paths and quality failures.\n- Improve indexing/cache behavior, retrieval and rank fusion, packet relevance/density, and delivery\n  integration as evidence warrants.\n- Require every candidate to pass regressions and advance the development frontier across all three\n  dimensions; document rejected candidates.\n\nExit: one stabilized candidate clears the planning effect floors with uncertainty on development data\nand has no critical-stratum regression.\n\n### Phase E — freeze and confirm\n\n- Recompute power from development-only calibration; freeze tasks, repetitions, effects, endpoint\n  rules, aggregation, K, engines, model/runner, prices, schedule, holdout commitment, budget, and\n  protocol hash.\n- Obtain explicit owner authorization for the paid run.\n- Execute the blinded fixed-cell holdout once, validate evidence, and run the locked analyzer.\n\nExit: the optimized treatment clears the joint superiority rule for time, token cost, and code quality.\nOtherwise return to Phase C or D with a new holdout; do not reinterpret the failed confirmatory set as\ndevelopment data.\n\n### Phase F — ship and reproduce\n\n- Land the validated product changes and telemetry.\n- Publish privacy-safe evidence and exact reproduction instructions.\n- Update the handover with final effects, confidence bounds, costs, limitations, and remaining risks.\n\nExit: an independent clean-checkout review reproduces the protocol verdict and the shipped product\nmatches the measured candidate.\nand relevance population. The hypothesis is that selecting or honestly omitting low-value memory can\npreserve the tasks where memory flips failure to success while avoiding the large time/token\nregressions seen on other development tasks. It remains a hypothesis until the four-arm causal design\nmeasures time, normalized model cost, and code quality jointly.\n\n### 9.16 Expanded multi-repository development inventory is frozen and integrated\n\nThe v2 inventory source commit `8d9b468e6a485df624b0681ab99a827e3d1254df` is integrated, with the\nsame tree, at `00642c67401e56aad8445cc3ba1e6e20a1976dad` on\n`codex/benchmark-repair-integration`. It binds exact Git, repository, remote/ref, 31-first-parent\nwindow, module/workspace, Go/native toolchain, path-set, diff, stable-patch, lineage, test-evidence,\nand package-target identities for 62 additional permanently development-only candidates:\n\n| Repository | Candidates | Low / medium / high |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Entire Brain | 18 | 5 / 7 / 6 |\n| Entire DB | 25 | 9 / 4 / 12 |\n| Entire Graph | 19 | 6 / 3 / 10 |\n| Total | 62 | 20 / 14 / 28 |\n\nThe set contains 37 single-parent integration units and 25 two-parent feature branches. Test evidence\nis 55 changed-Go-test-only, five changed-test-plus-fixture/helper, and two fixture/helper-only records.\nEvery candidate is `structurally_ready_not_executed`; every negative control is `not_executed`; every\nsource-session decision is unresolved. Structural readiness is not a causal task-validity result.\n\nThe global registry combines the 62 v2 records with the 23 existing CLI records for 85 development\nidentities: 27 low, 17 medium, and 41 high. It reports zero duplicate groups across commit, tree,\nsource/test/full diff SHA-256, and source/test/full stable patch ID. Repeated path commitments remain\nseparate diagnostics (one changed-path group, two source-path groups, one test-path group). Exact\nidentity does not resolve semantic, related-family, fix-lineage, or source-session overlap, and the\nregistry has no owner HMAC, population assignment, calibration/holdout membership, or run authority.\n\nThe independent release audit reproduced every candidate and the complete registry, exactly rehashed\nall 14 frozen files, passed all four repository-bound checks, and ran 19 focused tests in 61.644\nseconds. The parent reran the 19 tests in 64.875 seconds; the complete confirmatory suite passed 345\ntests in 82.404 seconds; targeted Pyright reported zero errors. Both reviews found no P0, P1, or P2\nfinding. They ran no scheduled frozen-candidate test, provider/model call, private-data read, fetch,\nor paid benchmark.\n\n### 9.17 Stage 1 freezes the six-arm packet tradeoff surface\n\nProduct commit `cfb43d66b255c7c351512e5eae17bc73aa3e7cb9` adds only\n`internal/cli/brain_brief_packet_measurement_test.go`; its file SHA-256 is\n`1ebf8d190edf54b9e097ffb93702eb9ff30f55428203818eaff56932f773f363`. The test fixes the synthetic\nreport fingerprint, exact wire hashes, byte/rune/lexeme counts, transparent utility result, format\nparsers, compact-v2 projection parity, `agent_v1` integrity and 32 KiB budget, always/shadow byte\nequality, structured-path privacy behavior, natural-language evidence preservation, and fail-safe\nshadow reasons.\n\nFrozen fixture results:\n\n| Arm | Bytes | Unicode runes | Lexemes v1 | Utility score (bp) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| CLI text default | 1,573 | 1,567 | 309 | 5,907 |\n| Legacy JSON | 12,220 | 12,220 | 2,995 | 10,000 |\n| `compact_v1` | 5,662 | 5,662 | 1,468 | 9,285 |\n| `compact_v2` | 5,356 | 5,356 | 1,385 | 9,285 |\n| `agent_v1` always | 2,486 | 2,486 | 508 | 8,228 |\n| `agent_v1` shadow | 2,486 | 2,486 | 508 | 8,228 |\n\nIndependent `go test ./internal/cli -count=1` passed in 101.376 seconds. The parent `go test ./...`\npassed with the CLI package at 104.188 seconds, and `go vet ./...` passed. Independent and parent\nreview found no P0, P1, or P2 finding.\n\nThese numbers are not a product-gain claim. They come from one deterministic handcrafted report. The\nutility score is weighted substring retention, not agent code quality; lexemes v1 is explicitly a\ntokenizer-independent lexical proxy, not model tokens; the benchmark times only serialization from a\nprebuilt report, not retrieval, report construction, or end-to-end elapsed time. It supplies no model\nor provider evidence and no task-success outcome. The exact hashes make contract changes reviewable,\nbut Stage 2 must validate any separately versioned packet/admission candidate on a larger\ndevelopment-only task and relevance population before a default, model trial, or user-value claim.\n\n### 9.18 Agent-v1 pressure precursor freezes the local cost defect\n\nProduct commit `31b7d0d73a9e7996b1cc02ada9a4f21cb7c76310` adds only\n`internal/cli/brain_brief_agent_v1_benchmark_test.go`, whose committed SHA-256 is\n`5f85dec308c02024969cacace1365b40f03e90f84fb81ed69690937bf81f0e4a`. It makes no production\nbehavior change. Four deterministic fixtures freeze the ordinary rich packet (2,486 bytes),\nnear-budget history truncation (32,071 bytes), section-prefix pressure (32,573 bytes), and a fixed\n10,000-rune UTF-8 fact (31,473 bytes), including exact report fingerprints, packet hashes, integrity,\nUTF-8 validity, the 32 KiB limit, emitter/builder/count parity, prefix ordering, residual\nlower-priority contribution, and mutation checks.\n\nThe precursor separates the production profiled path—emit the packet and then rebuild it to derive\ncounts—from one direct build. Repeated M4 benchmem samples showed essentially twice the memory and\nallocations in the profiled path:\n\n| Fixture | Profiled path | Direct builder |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Rich | ~321 KB / 1,189 allocs | ~160 KB / 594 allocs |\n| Near-budget history | ~9.585 MB / 5,170 allocs | ~4.793 MB / 2,586 allocs |\n| Section prefix | ~14.928 MB / 5,638 allocs | ~7.464 MB / 2,822 allocs |\n| Fixed UTF-8 | ~962 KB / 490 allocs | ~481 KB / 245 allocs |\n\nIndependent review found no P0–P2 issue; repeated focused tests, the CLI package, and CLI vet passed.\nTiming is only an integrated local proxy because the profiled arm includes dispatch and `io.Discard`\nwhile production writes through a `bytes.Buffer`. This commit identifies duplicate packet construction;\nit does not establish an end-to-end time, model-token, or code-quality result.\n\n### 9.19 Product slice A removes the profiled-path double build\n\nProduct commit `86f0d70d0e730142072450e48905def12c722013` changes exactly six CLI files. The profiled `agent_v1`\npath now returns counts from the exact projection that was successfully written instead of rebuilding\nthe packet. Preparation or writer failure returns zero counts, missing counts fail closed, non-agent\nprofiling is unchanged, profile/unprofiled and always/shadow bytes remain identical, and sidecar,\noutput, and receipt ordering is preserved. The packet packer itself is intentionally untouched, so\nthis slice isolates the duplicate-build repair.\n\nShort paired medians versus `31b7d0d7` reduced profiled-path time by 35.7% on rich, 50.7% on history\npressure, 49.8% on section-prefix pressure, and 48.0% on fixed UTF-8; bytes and allocations fell by\napproximately 50% on every fixture. The longer direct-builder sample retained the precursor allocation\nmedians exactly—594, 2,587, 2,823, and 245—confirming that this slice did not change the packer.\nEvery frozen packet hash remained byte-identical. Independent review found no P0–P2 issue; repeated\nregressions, `go test ./internal/cli -count=1`, and CLI vet passed.\n\nThe eliminated second build and allocation reduction are deterministic; the timing percentages are\nsupporting microbenchmark evidence and were noisy on the smallest fixture. This is a real local product\ncost reduction, but not an endpoint-level or agent-value claim.\n\n### 9.20 Product slice B packs agent packets incrementally\n\nProduct commit `9789ae7b32be402d192741477562703361cc8361` changes only\n`brain_brief_agent_v1.go` and its tests. It replaces repeated candidate-slice copies and complete footer\nserialization in the optional-record admission loop with a capped incremental `strings.Builder` and an\nexact footer-size encoder. Mandatory overflow remains a pre-write error; footer field order and exact\nlegacy bytes, fixed-width SHA-256 sizing, decimal/boolean widths, section-prefix behavior, lower-priority\nresidual contribution, privacy/integrity/config identities, output counts, and always/shadow parity are\nunchanged.\n\nThe formal 10x500ms direct-builder comparison versus Slice A reported:\n\n| Fixture | Time | Bytes/op | Allocs/op |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Rich | −41.5% | −63.7% | −39.6% |\n| Near-budget history | −65.4% | −87.1% | −43.7% |\n\nA separate independent-audit sample observed rich moving from 58.8 to 25.2 microseconds,\n160.5 to 58.2 KB/op, and 594 to 359 allocations, and near-budget history moving from 1.075 to\n0.408 milliseconds, 4.793 MB to 619 KB/op, and 2,587 to 1,456 allocations. Those raw figures are\ncorroborating sample evidence, not the formal 10x500ms result.\n\nThe admission-shadow evaluator stayed effectively flat at approximately 11.62 µs, 25.50 KB, and 102\nallocations versus the prior 11.42 µs, 25.48 KB, and 102 allocations. Independent audit found no\nP0–P2 issue; all agent-v1, shadow, Stage 1, pressure, sizing, diff, vet, and repeated test gates passed.\nThis remains the last pushed and audited Agent V1 checkpoint. The separately versioned, opt-in Agent\nV2 checkpoint is recorded below and does not alter Agent V1 bytes or defaults.\n\nThese are packet-emitter microbenchmarks over prebuilt synthetic reports. They do not measure retrieval,\nend-to-end agent time, billed/model tokens, task success, or downstream code quality, and they do not\nauthorize a default switch or model trial. They advance the local product frontier without satisfying\nthe three-dimensional finish line.\n\n### 9.20a Agent V2 is privacy-audited, pushed, and still opt-in\n\nThe separately versioned, always-only Agent V2 semantic-edge candidate is pushed at exact commit\n`77ace7bd306cf839154169ef79308a667f97c810` on `codex/agent-v2-semantic-edges`. It preserves Agent V1\nbytes and defaults. Independent final audit found P0=0, P1=0, and P2=0; focused V2 and V1 regressions,\nthe selected race suite, `go vet ./...`, and the full uncached Go suite passed. Five isolated\nserializer samples had medians of 42,364 ns/op, 74,963 B/op, and 444 allocations/op for the rich\nfixture, and 391,856 ns/op, 632,052 B/op, and 1,517 allocations/op for near-budget history.\n\nThis closes the candidate's privacy/canonicality audit and makes it a valid opt-in implementation\ncheckpoint. It is not end-to-end elapsed-time evidence, a model-token-cost measurement, a task-success\nresult, or downstream-code-quality evidence, and it does not authorize a default change or model run.\nThe technically green exact `o200k_base` tokenizer slice remains parked on its independent P2\nasset-specific redistribution-license finding and must rebase onto exact Agent V2 commit `77ace7bd`.\n\n### 9.21 Multi-repository negative-control execution is planned, not authorized\n\nSource commit `4a113d0f0fe08d5c7f1bae7767341cb8eb854e48` is integrated with the same tree at\n`1e5c95aae5f016a70183c219ac763a77e993debc` on `codex/benchmark-repair-integration`. The raw checked\nplan SHA-256 is `f55b6e22a25daf8016305304adf3b7ac8d31675281d97cfc9bb78cc76b619790` and its canonical\n`plan_sha256` is `a47311fa1f4553f8085ebea6e8ddd003a4ea5d0b2d269d91bbcdd414134a248e`.\nIt freezes all 62 ordered candidates, baseline before first-parent source reversal, two repetitions per\narm, serial execution, a 600-second process-group deadline per arm, and an eight-hour total ceiling.\nIt also freezes an 8 GiB staging ceiling plus an 8 GiB reserve, so execution requires evidence of at\nleast 16 GiB usable capacity before staging. macOS reports about 191.9 GB available for important\nusage, so the host is not capacity-blocked; the existing injected `statvfs` primitive does not model\nAPFS purgeable capacity or reserve it. The plan is\n`pending_owner_authorization` and the checked tool exposes only deterministic `build` and `check`\nlanes—no executor or candidate process.\n\nThe first independent audit blocked the slice on two P1 findings: the plan did not re-verify the\nglobal registry's current CLI scanner/canonical helper and Git identities, and the schema accepted\nrepository/candidate reorderings that the runtime checker rejected. The repaired implementation pins\nthose dependencies and embeds an exact generated schema projection of all three repository rows and\nall 62 ordered candidate references/positions. Final audit is P0=0, P1=0, P2=1. The remaining P2 is\ntest quality only: the noncanonical-byte test proves byte inequality but does not itself invoke the\nCLI rejection branch, although the checker rejects those bytes. Fourteen focused tests, 19 inventory/\nregistry regressions, targeted Pyright with zero errors, and six resealed schema-tamper probes passed.\n\nThe six execution-gate categories are:\n\n1. owner execution-approval receipt and exact trust mechanism;\n2. bounded content-addressed offline Go cache-seed manifest and archive;\n3. clean detached-worktree executor and first-parent-only reversal proof;\n4. complete classification truth table and receipt checker;\n5. private content-addressed raw-log writer and checker; and\n6. disk/resource preflight, fail-closed cleanup, and no-receipt-on-interruption attestation.\n\nCategory 5's private content-addressed raw-log writer/checker is independently audited and integrated\nthrough `157f805b`. Independently SAFE category 2/6 cache-seed/resource-validation primitives are\nintegrated at `f319e954`; they do not provide actual approved seed/archive material, an executor, an\nowner receipt, or execution authority. Category 4's classification truth table and receipt checker\nare independently SAFE in two audits and are committed, pushed, and integrated at `1c39aeb2` on both\n`codex/negative-control-classifier` and `codex/benchmark-repair-integration`. Nineteen focused tests,\n429 full confirmatory tests, the 242-test benchmark harness, and targeted Pyright with zero errors\npassed. The classifier binds exactly 248 scheduled attempts; none has run.\n\nNo real candidate, scheduled frozen-candidate test, worktree reversal, model/provider, private-data,\nholdout, or paid run occurred. Calibration/holdout membership, population assignment, and all execution\nauthority remain explicitly absent or forbidden.\n\n### 9.22 Historical 2026-07-17 proposed sequence and blockers\n\nThis sequence was proposed at the 2026-07-17 checkpoint. It is retained as part\nof the engineering log, not as current execution guidance. In particular, it\ndoes not authorize the scheduled attempts. Use the 2026-07-18 handover for the\ncurrent ordered moves.\n\nThe unpaid work proposed at that checkpoint was:\n\n1. Establish the authenticated owner trust root and obtain the exact execution-approval receipt.\n2. Bind the actual approved content-addressed offline cache seed and archive; the integrated category\n   2 validation primitives alone are insufficient.\n3. Complete and independently audit the clean detached-worktree executor and first-parent reversal,\n   the attested attempt producer and private-log integration, and fail-closed cleanup/interruption\n   behavior. Add a trusted APFS-aware important-usage capacity observation and bounded staging\n   reservation/cleanup attestation for the exact 16 GiB rule before staging.\n4. Only after steps 1–3 pass, execute the already frozen and classifier-bound 248 attempts under that\n   explicit authorization; do not regenerate the inventory from outcomes.\n5. Independently write and review symptom prompts only for negative-control survivors. Close semantic,\n   related-family, fix-lineage, and source-session overlap under authenticated authority; retain the\n   resulting pool as development-only.\n6. Resolve the exact `o200k_base` tokenizer slice's asset-specific redistribution-license P2 and\n   rebase it onto exact Agent V2 commit `77ace7bd`. Preserve all `agent_v1` bytes/defaults, keep both\n   changes opt-in, and do not optimize solely to the synthetic score.\n7. Expand and authenticate the development relevance/task evidence, including the safe-exported query\n   fixture and actual producer/catalog/engine/source/output bindings. Evaluate packet/admission choices\n   on development data under the four-arm contract; do not open the holdout.\n8. Obtain owner-approved restricted engine retention, quality rubric and critical-stratum bounds,\n   development calibration and powered counts, runner/model/prices, and budget. Seal the representative\n   commitment-only holdout and freeze the canonical protocol before requesting any fixed-cell paid\n   confirmatory execution.\n\nThe blockers are explicit: the pending-only plan has a SAFE classifier/receipt contract but no owner\ntrust/approval, actual approved seed/archive binding, executor/reversal path, attested\nproducer/private-log integration, cleanup attestation, or run authority. The host has about 191.9 GB\navailable for important usage, but the trusted APFS-aware observer and bounded reservation required\nto attest the exact 16 GiB rule remain absent. The 62 candidates lack negative-control and\nprompt-review results; the 85-identity registry lacks authenticated semantic/family/session authority; the\n14-query/13-task relevance set is too small and not authoritatively bound for a default choice;\nStage 1 has no model-token, end-to-end-time, or agent-code-quality outcome; durable engine evidence and publication authority are\npending; owner trust, population/split, calibration/power, quality, runner/pricing, and budget approvals\nare absent; and fresh holdout plaintext must remain unopened. Development implementation can continue\ninside those boundaries. Confirmatory execution cannot.\n\n## 10. Guardrails and authorization boundaries\n\n- Do not open or derive the fresh holdout before protocol freeze.\n- Do not run paid model benchmarks without explicit owner authorization and a passing pricing/budget\n  gate.\n- Do not publish v3 plaintext, private facts, raw session identifiers, secrets, host paths, raw semantic\n  vectors, or redistributable model bytes without a completed privacy and license review.\n- Do not improve the benchmark by weakening task difficulty, validation, baselines, or missing-data\n  policy.\n- Do not call a result complete because an average improves while a co-primary confidence bound or\n  critical safety gate fails.\n- Keep the program active when infrastructure is ready but product gains are not yet proven.\n\n## 11. Owner decisions still required before confirmatory freeze\n\nDevelopment can continue without these decisions, but confirmatory freeze cannot:\n\n- provision and approve the authenticated owner trust root used to sign/verify candidate, population,\n  calibration, and final owner receipts;\n- approve the final claim floors, strictly better finite/domain-valid planning alternatives, and the\n  calibrated or explicitly conservative assumptions used to meet the frozen 0.80 marginal/joint power\n  target;\n- approve the exact quality rubric and critical-stratum regression bounds;\n- approve the independently reviewed task population and commitment-only calibration/holdout split;\n- authorize the one-time non-holdout safe export of the 14 development queries and temporal policies,\n  plus independent receipt review and pinning;\n- authorize any development model calibration that incurs cost;\n- approve encrypted private-evidence storage and key custody, if exact v3 retention is required;\n- approve the model/runner, current prices, maximum agent-invocation envelope, and budget cap; and\n- authorize the one-time paid holdout execution.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"5dfd74e8-4c67-4fb7-a4c3-45f47a5e848d"}}}
